s `ard:worker:<role>`); closes out through the shared Finisher\n internal/labels/            the one place that spells an `ard:` label; catalogue from config\n internal/labels/labelsync/  `ard labels sync`: diff catalogue vs repo, create/update via effects\n-internal/setupcmd/          `ard setup`: checks, then applies (labels) when green; --check, --dry-run\n+internal/setupcmd/          `ard setup`: checks, then applies (labels, ARD_BOT_LOGIN) when green; --check, --dry-run, --print-workflow; workflow/ holds the embedded bot-commits.yml copy\n internal/gitref/            ref-name checks shared by config and executor; leaf, stdlib only\n internal/repopath/          owner/repo to lowercased, nested local path segments (state, lock, work, logs); leaf, stdlib only\n internal/initcmd/           ard init: proposes the starter .ard/ as a PR on ard-init\ndiff --git a/docs/proposal.md b/docs/proposal.md\nindex c7c1a69..7e1aa04 100644\n--- a/docs/proposal.md\n+++ b/docs/proposal.md\n@@ -516,7 +516,7 @@ What `true` and `false` mean per executor, as the code does today: the host exec\n \n **`ard init` creates and upgrades `.ard/` (decided, #27; revised, #303).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/lts`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lts, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), then brings `.ard/` up to them. By default it writes the **local working tree** for a human to commit; with `--pr` it commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR, as before. Either way a human commits or merges: ard never writes `main`, and `scan` and `aid` are written `100755`. **Upgrades** are tracked in `.ard/ard-init.lock`, a YAML file holding the sha256 of every file init created or updated; init adds or refreshes a file's entry whenever it writes that file. The user may edit the lock; ard uses it only to decide whether a file changed since init wrote it. Per file: absent, create it; identical to the new starter, leave it; hash equal to its lock entry, update it; anything else (including no lock or no entry) counts as edited and is never overwritten: init lists it with the starter's diff. A file the starter dropped is listed, never deleted, and init writes nothing outside `.ard/`. The three repo context templates (`.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md`) are create-if-absent: written only when missing, never treated as an upgrade and never overwritten; the next steps tell an adopter with an existing config to add their paths to `merge.protected_paths`. In local mode init refuses while `.ard/` has uncommitted changes; in `--pr` mode it refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits. If nothing differs it prints `nothing to change`. Init runs the shared permission check (#302: repo reachable, App installed, the permissions ard needs) and reports it; a failed check warns in local mode and fails in `--pr` mode. It no longer syncs labels: that moved to `ard setup`. With `--dry-run` it prints the plan and writes nothing (local mode) or logs the commit, push and PR to the ledger (`--pr`). It ends by naming the next steps: commit, merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit** (never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history in `--pr` mode; **Rebase and merge** is also safe, #85), then `ard setup`. When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed.\n \n-**`ard setup` applies `.ard/` to GitHub (decided, #303).** It reads the local `./.ard/` and warns when it differs from `origin/main`, which `ard serve` runs. With no flags it runs every check, then applies only if all of them are green; `--check` reports only (exit 2 on drift) and `--dry-run` routes writes through the effect layer. Each row is `ok`, `changed`, `drift` or `manual` with the exact fix. It **writes** only two things: the `ard:*` label catalogue (create and update, never delete; `ard labels sync` stays as an alias) and the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`, derived from the App's own identity (`<slug>[bot]`), never from config. Variables need the operator's token (`gh auth token` or a PAT, #302), read once, held in memory and never logged, stored or passed to an agent or job. It **only checks**, against a policy built into ard rather than repo content, and prints the fix on drift: the shared permission check; `main`'s active ruleset requires a pull request and its bypass list is exactly the ard App in mode Always; the App has no `workflows` or `administration` permission; merge settings (`allow_rebase_merge` off once versioning is on); and `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` on `origin/main` against a copy embedded in ard (`ard setup --print-workflow` prints it for a human to commit). Setup never commits to the repository, `.github/` included, and never writes rulesets, bypass actors or App settings: a merged config change must not be able to loosen branch protection through an admin token. `ard setup --create-gh-app` (#350) creates the App itself with GitHub's manifest flow.\n+**`ard setup` applies `.ard/` to GitHub (decided, #303).** It reads the local `./.ard/` and warns when it differs from `origin/main`, which `ard serve` runs. With no flags it runs every check, then applies only if all of them are green; `--check` reports only (exit 2 on drift) and `--dry-run` routes writes through the effect layer. Each row is `ok`, `changed`, `drift` or `manual` with the exact fix. It **writes** only two things: the `ard:*` label catalogue (create and update, never delete; `ard labels sync` stays as an alias) and the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`, derived from the App's own identity (`<slug>[bot]`), never from config; a different existing value is replaced only after a yes at a terminal prompt (otherwise the row is `manual` with the `gh variable set` command). Variables need the operator's token (`gh auth token` or a PAT, #302), read once, held in memory and never logged, stored or passed to an agent or job. It **only checks**, against a policy built into ard rather than repo content, and prints the fix on drift: the shared permission check; `main`'s active ruleset requires a pull request and its bypass list is exactly the ard App in mode Always; the App has no `workflows` or `administration` permission; merge settings (`allow_rebase_merge` off once versioning is on); and `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` on `origin/main` against a copy embedded in ard (`ard setup --print-workflow` prints it, and nothing else, for a human to commit; a missing, different or unreadable file is a `manual` row that does not block the label sync). Setup never commits to the repository, `.github/` included, and never writes rulesets, bypass actors or App settings: a merged config change must not be able to loosen branch protection through an admin token. `ard setup --create-gh-app` (#350) creates the App itself with GitHub's manifest flow.\n \n **Where `ard serve` reads config (decided, #87).** After each fetch into its own clone (`~/.ard/repos/<owner>/<repo>.git`, see Local-executor details) `ard serve` resolves `origin/main` to a SHA and reads `config.yaml` and the role directories from that commit, not from the working tree. It exports them into a snapshot at `~/.ard/config/<owner>/<repo>/<sha>/`, kept 24 hours after a newer SHA replaces it, because roles run from a host path: a role directory, its `Dockerfile` and the scan worktree must all describe the one commit the tick's jobs use. Editing the host's working tree therefore changes nothing on a running controller, with one exception: a local `paused: true` is honoured (see the kill switch), and only that key is read locally. A merged change applies on the next tick without a pull. Symlinks and submodules under the role directories are rejected, and a config that cannot be fetched or does not validate counts as paused. If `origin/main` cannot be loaded at startup, `ard serve` starts paused instead of exiting, posts the load error to the status issue once per distinct error, and retries every tick; no loop runs until a config has loaded. `ard config check` validates the working tree, for authoring; `ard config check --ref <ref>` validates a commit of the local checkout (for example `--ref origin/main` after `git fetch`) without fetching.\n \ndiff --git a/docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md b/docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md\nindex 1ce4d2e..c85ce51 100644\n--- a/docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md\n+++ b/docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md\n@@ -46,8 +46,9 @@ Each `manual: <name>: <fix>` row carries the exact fix. Typical ones:\n   `main`; see \"Branch protection and the bot\" in the proposal.\n - `merge-settings`: turn off rebase merging.\n - Bot-commit audit, by hand in the target repository (do not edit `.github/`\n-  here): copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` from this repository, then set\n-  the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login.\n+  here): run `ard setup --print-workflow > .github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and open a PR;\n+  `ard setup` sets the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login\n+  (it asks before replacing a different value).\n \n Rerun `ard setup` until it is green.\n \ndiff --git a/docs/site/getting-started.md b/docs/site/getting-started.md\nindex 806815d..fffb73a